Here is a summary of the changes for each section of the bill, starting with Sec. 100001.

**Sec. 100001. Applicability of the immigration laws.** This section establishes that the fees created under this subtitle apply to aliens in the described circumstances and that terms used have the meanings given in section 101 of the Immigration and Nationality Act. It also clarifies that any reference to a section or provision in this subtitle is a reference to the Immigration and Nationality Act unless expressly provided otherwise.

**Sec. 100002. Asylum fee.** This section requires aliens filing an asylum application to pay a fee of the greater of $100 or an amount established by rule, adjusted annually for inflation. It mandates that 50% of fees collected by the Attorney General be credited to the Executive Office for Immigration Review and 50% of fees collected by the Secretary of Homeland Security be credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, with no fee waivers permitted.

**Sec. 100003. Employment authorization document fees.** This section establishes a $550 fee for initial employment authorization applications filed by asylum applicants, parolees, and aliens granted temporary protected status, subject to annual inflation adjustments. It specifies that 25% of fees from asylum applicants are credited to USCIS, while all fees from parolees and temporary protected status holders are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury, with no fee waivers allowed.

**Sec. 100004. Immigration parole fee.** This section requires aliens paroled into the United States to pay a fee of the greater of $1,000 or an amount established by rule, adjusted annually for inflation. It provides exceptions for medical emergencies, organ donation, imminent family death, funeral attendance, and certain law enforcement purposes, and deposits all collected fees into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100005. Special immigrant juvenile fee.** This section requires aliens, parents, or legal guardians applying for special immigrant juvenile status to pay a fee of the greater of $250 or an amount established by rule, adjusted annually for inflation. All fees collected are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100006. Temporary protected status fee.** This section amends section 244(c)(1)(B) of the Immigration and Nationality Act to increase the maximum fee for temporary protected status from $50 to $500, subject to annual inflation adjustments. It also requires all fees collected to be deposited into the general fund of the Treasury and prohibits fee waivers.

**Sec. 100007. Visa integrity fee.** This section requires aliens issued a nonimmigrant visa to pay a fee of the greater of $250 or an amount established by rule, adjusted annually for inflation. It allows the Secretary of Homeland Security to reimburse the fee if the alien complies with all visa conditions and departs timely or adjusts status, with unreimbursed fees deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100008. Form I-94 fee.** This section requires aliens submitting an application for a Form I-94 Arrival/Departure Record to pay a fee of the greater of $24 or an amount established by rule, adjusted annually for inflation. It directs 20% of collected fees to the Land Border Inspection Fee Account for U.S. Customs and Border Protection processing, with remaining amounts deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100009. Annual asylum fee.** This section requires aliens with pending asylum applications to pay an annual fee of the greater of $100 or an amount established by rule for each calendar year the application remains pending, adjusted annually for inflation. All fees collected are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury, with no fee waivers permitted.

**Sec. 100010. Fee relating to renewal and extension of employment authorization for parolees.** This section requires parolees seeking renewal or extension of employment authorization to pay a fee of the greater of $275 or an amount established by rule, adjusted annually for inflation. It specifies that 25% of fees collected are credited to USCIS and deposited into the Immigration Examinations Fee Account, with remaining amounts deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100011. Fee relating to renewal or extension of employment authorization for asylum applicants.** This section requires asylum applicants to pay a fee of not less than $275 for each renewal or extension of employment authorization. It provides that employment authorization terminates upon denial of the asylum application by an asylum officer, immigration judge, or the Board of Immigration Appeals, and directs 25% of fees to USCIS with remaining amounts to the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100012. Fee relating to renewal and extension of employment authorization for aliens granted temporary protected status.** This section requires aliens granted temporary protected status to pay a fee of the greater of $275 or an amount established by rule for renewal or extension of employment authorization, adjusted annually for inflation. It directs 25% of fees collected to USCIS and the Immigration Examinations Fee Account, with remaining amounts deposited into the general fund of the Treasury, and prohibits fee waivers.

**Sec. 100013. Fees relating to applications for adjustment of status.** This section establishes numerous fees for immigration court filings, including $1,500 for adjustment of status, $1,050 for waiver of inadmissibility, $500 for temporary protected status, $900 for appeals and motions, $600 for suspension of deportation, and $1,500 for cancellation of removal and adjustment of status. It directs up to 25% of collected fees to the Executive Office for Immigration Review and prohibits use of funds for the Legal Orientation Program.

**Sec. 100014. Electronic System for Travel Authorization fee.** This section amends section 217(h)(3)(B) of the Immigration and Nationality Act to require a fee of not less than $10 and not less than $13 per travel authorization, extending the authority through October 31, 2034. It also requires annual inflation adjustments for fiscal year 2026 and each subsequent fiscal year.

**Sec. 100015. Electronic Visa Update System fee.** This section requires aliens subject to the Electronic Visa Update System to pay a fee of the greater of $30 or an amount established by rule, adjusted annually for inflation. It establishes a CBP Electronic Visa Update System Account in the Treasury for deposited fees, appropriates amounts for U.S. Customs and Border Protection costs, and deposits $5 per payment into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100016. Fee for aliens ordered removed in absentia.** This section requires aliens ordered removed in absentia who are subsequently arrested by U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement to pay a fee of the greater of $5,000 or an amount established by rule, adjusted annually for inflation. It credits 50% of collected fees to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement's Detention and Removal Office Fee Account, with remaining amounts deposited into the general fund of the Treasury, and provides an exception for rescinded orders.

**Sec. 100017. Inadmissible alien apprehension fee.** This section requires inadmissible aliens apprehended between ports of entry to pay a fee of the greater of $5,000 or an amount established by rule, adjusted annually for inflation. It credits 50% of collected fees to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement's Detention and Removal Office Fee Account, with remaining amounts deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100018. Amendment to authority to apply for asylum.** This section amends section 208(d)(3) of the Immigration and Nationality Act to change "may" to "shall" regarding fee establishment and removes the limitation that fees shall not exceed a certain amount. It clarifies that nothing in the paragraph limits the Attorney General's authority to set additional adjudication and naturalization fees under section 286(m).

**Sec. 100051. Appropriation for the Department of Homeland Security.** This section appropriates $2,055,000,000 to the Secretary of Homeland Security for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029, for immigration enforcement activities including hiring agents, departures and removals, personnel assignments, background checks, protecting alien children, transporting aliens from contiguous territory, state and local participation, removal of specified unaccompanied alien children, expedited removal of criminal aliens, criminal and gang checks, and information technology.

**Sec. 100052. Appropriation for U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement.** This section appropriates $29,850,000,000 to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029, for hiring and training, performance and retention bonuses, recruitment, transportation, information technology, facility upgrades, fleet modernization, family unity, 287(g) agreements, the Victims of Immigration Crime Engagement Office, and the Office of the Principal Legal Advisor.

**Sec. 100053. Appropriation for Federal Law Enforcement Training Centers.** This section appropriates $750,000,000 to the Federal Law Enforcement Training Centers for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029, with not less than $285,000,000 for training newly hired Federal law enforcement personnel and not more than $465,000,000 for facilities procurement, construction, maintenance, and training equipment.

**Sec. 100054. Appropriation for the Department of Justice.** This section appropriates $3,330,000,000 to the Attorney General for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029, for hiring immigration judges (capped at 800 effective November 1, 2028), combating drug trafficking, prosecuting immigration matters, litigation support, increasing funding for the Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Grant Program and Office of Community Oriented Policing, fiscally responsible lawsuit settlements, and compensation for incarceration of criminal aliens.

**Sec. 100055. Bridging Immigration-related Deficits Experienced Nationwide Reimbursement Fund.** This section establishes the BIDEN Reimbursement Fund within the Department of Justice to provide grants to eligible States and local governments for locating and apprehending criminal aliens, collecting law enforcement information, investigating and prosecuting crimes by aliens and drug/human trafficking, court operations, temporary detention, transportation, and vehicle maintenance. It appropriates not to exceed $3,500,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2028, for qualified expenses incurred on or after January 20, 2021, distributed to more than one State.

**Sec. 100056. Appropriation for the Bureau of Prisons.** This section appropriates $5,000,000,000 to the Director of the Bureau of Prisons for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029, with not less than $3,000,000,000 for hiring and training new employees and salaries and benefits for the current workforce, and not more than $2,000,000,000 for maintenance and repairs to facilities.

**Sec. 100057. Appropriation for the United States Secret Service.** This section appropriates $1,170,000,000 to the Director of the United States Secret Service for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029, for additional resources including personnel, training facilities, programming, and technology, and for performance, retention, and signing bonuses for qualified personnel. It requires written service agreements for bonuses specifying commencement and termination dates, bonus amounts, and conditions for termination.

**Sec. 100101. Appropriation to the Administrative Office of the United States Courts.** This section appropriates $1,250,000 for each of fiscal years 2025 through 2028 to the Director of the Administrative Office of the United States Courts for continuing analyses and reporting under section 604(a)(2) of title 28, including assessments of judicial orders issuing non-party relief against the Federal Government and their aggregate cost impact on taxpayers.

**Sec. 100102. Appropriation to the Federal Judicial Center.** This section appropriates $1,000,000 for each of fiscal years 2025 through 2028 to the Director of the Federal Judicial Center for continuing education and training programs for judicial branch personnel, including training on the absence of constitutional and statutory authority for legal claims seeking non-party relief against the Federal Government and strategies for mitigating aggregate cost impact on taxpayers.

**Sec. 100201. Extension of fund.** This section amends section 3(d) of the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to extend the Fund's termination date to December 31, 2028, and strikes language referring to "the end of that 2-year period," inserting "such date" instead.

**Sec. 100202. Claims relating to atmospheric testing.** This section amends the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to expand leukemia and specified disease claims for individuals physically present in affected areas for at least one year between September 24, 1944, and November 6, 1962, increasing compensation to $100,000. It redefines "affected area" to include New Mexico, Utah, Idaho, specific Nevada counties, and Arizona counties, with New Mexico only for certain claims, and prohibits payment if previously made.

**Sec. 100203. Claims relating to uranium mining.** This section amends the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to expand eligibility for uranium mining claims to employees in mines or mills in Colorado, New Mexico, Arizona, Wyoming, South Dakota, Washington, Utah, Idaho, North Dakota, Oregon, or Texas between January 1, 1942, and December 31, 1990. It adds renal cancer and other chronic renal diseases as compensable, includes core drillers and ore transporters, allows combined work histories, and defines "core driller."

**Sec. 100204. Claims relating to Manhattan Project waste.** This section inserts a new section 5A into the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to provide compensation for claims relating to Manhattan Project waste, requiring physical presence in affected areas for at least two years after January 1, 1949, and contraction of a specified disease. It provides $50,000 or greater compensation for living individuals, additional compensation for documented out-of-pocket medical expenses up to December 31, 2028, and $25,000 for surviving spouses or children of deceased individuals, with affected areas defined by ZIP codes in Missouri, Tennessee, Alaska, and Kentucky.

**Sec. 100205. Limitations on claims.** This section amends section 8(a) of the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to extend the limitations period for claims from "2 years after the date of enactment of the RECA Extension Act of 2022" to December 31, 2027. [end of text]


